+## Native terminal surface
+
+`<terminal>` is a low-level fixed-cell painter for applications that already own a VT parser and PTY. A whole terminal frame crosses the React bridge as one `frame` prop and remains one retained host node. GPUI paints exact background rectangles and shapes each style run with a forced cell advance, so long box-drawing runs cannot drift away from the final column.
+
+```tsx
+<terminal
+  frame={{
+    cols: 80,
+    rows: 24,
+    cellWidth: 7.83,
+    lineHeight: 17,
+    fontSize: 13,
+    background: '#0b0c0c',
+    cursorColor: '#e7e7e7',
+    cursorX: 0,
+    cursorY: 0,
+    cursorVisible: true,
+    styles: [{
+      color: '#e7e7e7',
+      backgroundColor: '#0b0c0c',
+      fontFamily: 'Menlo',
+      fontWeight: 400,
+      italic: false,
+      underline: false,
+      strike: false,
+      fill: false,
+    }],
+    // row, column, column count, style index, text
+    runs: [[0, 0, 6, 0, 'prompt']],
+  }}
+  style={{ width: 626.4, height: 408, overflow: 'hidden' }}
+/>
+```
+
+The painter uses GPUI's cached monochrome glyph atlas (`MonochromeSprite`) for ordinary text, so foreground colour is applied to reusable alpha coverage on the GPU. Emoji remain on GPUI's polychrome atlas unless the application requests a text-presentation glyph. Call the optional `renderer.supportsNativeTerminal()` capability before selecting this surface when supporting older GPUIX binaries.
+
